From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Michael C. Ferguson" <michael.christopher.ferguson@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ARM: dts: socfpga: de10nano: reg-bearing UIO nodes for the LW
 window and the DDR3 aperture

Adds two interrupt-less, reg-bearing UIO nodes so the MiSTer HAL can map
the FPGA register window and the shared DDR3 aperture through named file
descriptors instead of /dev/mem.

Rationale
---------
Every FPGA-side mapping today goes through /dev/mem: an unnamed,
unbounded handle to all of physical memory, opened with O_SYNC as its
only attribute knob.  Two nodes replace that with named, size-bounded
descriptors -- /dev/uioN plus /sys/class/uio/uioN/maps/mapN/size -- so
the window a process can reach is the window the device tree gave it,
and a mapping bug lands as an mmap failure instead of as a write into
whatever else lives at that physical address.

The second reason is throughput, and it needs the companion kernel patch
0045 to become real.  The measured floor for the strongly-ordered
mapping /dev/mem can produce here is ~100 MB/s write and ~54 MB/s read
over the AXI bridge -- 23.3 us for a 2352-byte sector, measured on
silicon.  That floor is a property of the page attribute, not of the
bridge.

Two nodes, not one
------------------
The write-combining property below is node-scoped, and the safety
argument wants the LW window to be structurally incapable of inheriting
it rather than merely configured not to.  Hence one never-WC node for
the register window and one WC-capable node for the bulk aperture:

  mister_lw_window     0xff200000 + 0x200000   the whole 2 MB lwhps2fpga
                       window.  Mapped noncached (strongly ordered) by
                       uio_mmap_physical unconditionally -- which is what
                       control registers and the GPO strobe handshake
                       require, so the driver's lack of a choice here is
                       the correct behaviour and not a limitation.

  mister_ddr_aperture  0x20000000 + 0x20000000  the 512 MiB DDR3 aperture
                       shared with the FPGA over f2sdram.  Carries
                       "mister,map-writecombine".

The never-WC rule on the register window is not only about ordering.
Write-combining is Normal memory (patch 0045 quotes the encoding), and
ARMv7 permits speculative data accesses to Normal memory while forbidding
them to Device and Strongly-ordered memory.  A WC mapping of a 2 MB
window where an undecoded read hard-hangs the board (H-1 below) would
therefore let the CPU issue such a read on its own, off a mispredicted
path, with no program access at any bad offset at all -- turning "mapping
is safe, only probing is destructive" into a latent random hang.  Mapping
this window is safe precisely BECAUSE it is strongly ordered.  That is
the load-bearing half of the rule; the ordering requirement below is the
other half, and satisfying the ordering half alone is not sufficient.

Why the aperture is one node with one map, rather than a node per named
region: UIO selects a map by page offset and always maps from the
region's base, so a consumer reaching a high region maps a long prefix
of address space.  That cost is VA only -- the mappings are VM_PFNMAP,
so they consume no RAM and mlockall does not page-lock them -- and if it
ever proves to matter on device, the fix is to split the reg into up to
MAX_UIO_MAPS (5) entries at the hot region bases.  The property stays
node-scoped either way.  Per-region bounds enforcement stays in
userspace regardless, because a core's AXI windows are negotiated at
runtime and a static DT cannot enumerate them.

Write-combining is a two-part unlock
------------------------------------
This kernel's uio_mmap_physical maps UIO_MEM_PHYS with unconditional
pgprot_noncached (drivers/uio/uio.c), i.e. strongly ordered on ARMv7, so
this DTS change alone changes no mapping attribute anywhere.
"mister,map-writecombine" is read by nothing until patch 0045 (a
UIO_MEM_PHYS_WC memtype plus property parsing in uio_pdrv_genirq) is
applied; without it the property is an unknown DT property, ignored, and
the node maps at today's floor.

The fail-safe direction is deliberate.  An old kernel with a new DTB
degrades to slow-and-correct.  It never degrades to a CACHED mapping:
the FPGA writes this memory over f2sdram without snooping the A9 L1/L2,
so a cacheable mapping would produce silent data corruption rather than
lost throughput.  Write-combining is Normal non-cacheable -- stores
merge in the store buffer, nothing is allocated in cache -- which is
weaker ordering but not weaker coherency.

/dev/mem cannot provide this at all on this board, whatever flags are
passed.  The kernel's memory is capped at mem=511M on the boot command
line (docs/boot-chain.md section 6.4), so both windows here are outside
the kernel memory map, and for !pfn_valid ranges ARM's
phys_mem_access_prot() returns pgprot_noncached unconditionally.  The
O_SYNC-selects-write-combining path in that function applies only to
pfn_valid ranges -- and if this aperture ever became one, dropping
O_SYNC would hand back a *cached* mapping, i.e. exactly the corruption
class above.  That road is a dead end in both directions; the UIO path
is the only sanctioned one that does not invent a new driver.

mem=511M is also a PRECONDITION, not just an explanation
--------------------------------------------------------
The paragraph above cites the cap in the negative direction ("so /dev/mem
cannot help").  It runs in the positive direction too, and that is the
safety-relevant one: the DTS still declares memory@0 reg = <0x0
0x40000000>, a full 1 GiB that nominally covers this entire aperture.
The only thing keeping these pages out of the kernel's linear map is the
mem=511M token U-Boot supplies -- stock CONFIG_BOOTARGS, which this repo
does not own (docs/boot-chain.md:155 and section 6.4; memmap= is inert on
ARM, mem= does all of the work).

If that token were ever lost, or the memory node changed, the
write-combining mapping this patch enables would become a Normal
non-cacheable user alias of pages the kernel also maps cacheable.  On
ARMv7 mismatched-attribute aliases of the same physical page are
architecturally UNPREDICTABLE -- worse than the cached-mapping hazard
argued against above, and not fail-safe in any direction.  Exposure today
is low (boot-chain.md:876 lists mem=511M as CI-gated and user-immutable,
and the /dev/mem path this replaces has the same latent dependency), so
this is stated rather than enforced.  The structural fix, if it is ever
wanted, is a /reserved-memory node with no-map over 0x20000000 +
0x20000000, which would stop depending on a bootarg this repo does not
control.

No interrupts on purpose
------------------------
uio_pdrv_genirq accepts an interrupt-less node: platform_get_irq_optional
returns -ENXIO, the driver substitutes UIO_IRQ_NONE and probes normally
(drivers/uio/uio_pdrv_genirq.c).  These nodes carry memory only; the
doorbell nodes of patch 0043 carry interrupts only.  Keeping the two
families orthogonal means either can gain properties without disturbing
the other.

Map-only, never probe (hardware finding H-1)
--------------------------------------------
Mapping mister_lw_window is safe.  Reading an offset inside it that no
fabric slave decodes is not: the interconnect waits indefinitely for a
response and the HPS hard-hangs -- no bus fault, no exception, no panic,
no console output, no crash record, power-cycle-only recovery.  This was
confirmed three times on hardware, once with a serial console attached
that logged nothing at all.  The menu core decodes nothing anywhere in
this window.

All three confirmed events were READS, and that is a limit of the
evidence, not a scope limit on the rule.  No write to an undecoded LW
offset has ever been issued on hardware; a write also awaits a fabric
response through this strongly-ordered mapping, so it is expected to hang
identically and must be presumed equally lethal.  The rule covers every
access type -- do not read the measured half as licence for a
"harmless", write-only probe.

The node therefore changes no obligation on the consumer: it may touch
only offsets a loaded core has declared, and "probe the window to see
what answers" is a destructive operation -- in diagnostics too, which is
where the temptation lives.  A SIGBUS guard cannot help, because no
fault is ever delivered.  This is called out in the node comment as well
as here, because the DTS is where someone will read it first.

Mappings must be MAP_SHARED
---------------------------
UIO does not check the mapping flags: there is no VM_SHARED or
VM_MAYSHARE test anywhere in drivers/uio/uio.c, so a MAP_PRIVATE mmap of
either node reaches remap_pfn_range(), whose COW special case accepts a
full-VMA private mapping and installs write-protected PTEs.  Reads then
return real FPGA-side memory while the first write copies the page into
anonymous RAM, after which every store lands somewhere the FPGA can never
see.  A consumer that fences correctly and rings a doorbell publishes
nothing, the failure is data-dependent and non-crashing, and it survives
any read-back-through-the-same-mapping self-test.  This is pre-existing
UIO (and /dev/mem) behaviour rather than anything this patch introduces,
but the node comment is where the mapping contract is being written down,
so the flag is pinned there.

Node names are ABI, and the unit address does not change them
-------------------------------------------------------------
Userspace matches /sys/class/uio/uioN/name, never the uioN minor --
adding UIO nodes renumbers every minor after them, so the name is the
only stable handle.  Unlike the 0043 doorbell nodes, these have "reg", so
DT correctness wants a unit address regardless, and dtc's
unit_address_vs_reg check warns without one -- default-on in standalone
dtc, and in a kernel build only under W=1, since scripts/Makefile.dtbs
adds -Wno-unit_address_vs_reg unless KBUILD_EXTRA_WARN contains 1.

Both are satisfied at once with "linux,uio-name": uio_pdrv_genirq uses
that property verbatim for the exported name and falls back to the
fwnode name ("%pfwP", which includes the unit address) only when the
property is absent.  So the nodes carry proper unit addresses and export
the bare strings "mister_lw_window" and "mister_ddr_aperture".  This
also makes the name ABI explicit rather than an artifact of how the node
happens to be spelled -- worth having in its own right.  Renaming a node
is now safe; changing the property is not.

Placement
---------
Both nodes are children of the root, beside the doorbell nodes, and not
of soc/bus@ff200000 (the &fpga label).  That node is documented in the
base DTS as the place where an FPGA image's own device-tree overlay adds
its devices; nothing here should depend on it or collide with it.  The
overlapping reg range costs nothing: neither simple-bus nor
uio_pdrv_genirq requests the memory region, both only record it, so
there is no reservation to conflict over.

Binding, and why this patch is beta-local
-----------------------------------------
compatible = "generic-uio" rides the same mechanism patch 0043 set up:
uio_pdrv_genirq's of_match_table is empty until the of_id parameter
fills it, and board/mister/de10nano/linux-rt.fragment supplies
CONFIG_UIO=y, CONFIG_UIO_PDRV_GENIRQ=y and
CONFIG_CMDLINE="uio_pdrv_genirq.of_id=generic-uio" (with
CONFIG_CMDLINE_EXTEND=y) for the RT kernel.  No new kernel config symbol
is needed for this patch or for 0045.

Like 0043, this file lives only in linux-patches-beta/ and is listed
only in that directory's series file.  The shared linux-patches/
directory has no series file -- the stock 6.18 build applies all of it
via BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_PATCH -- so a copy there would reach the stock
kernel's DTS.  Beta-local is what keeps the stock build byte-identical.

Provenance
----------
Origin:       None.  Originally authored for this tree; no MiSTer fork
              commit corresponds to it.
Author:       Michael C. Ferguson, 2026-08-14.
Upstream:     No, and not upstreamable as-is.  Both "generic-uio" and
              "mister,map-writecombine" are per-system inventions rather
              than documented bindings, for the same reason 0043 is:
              "generic-uio" only ever matches because the of_id cmdline
              parameter writes it into an otherwise empty match table.
              Disposition "carry" (docs/patch-provenance.md 3.1).
Depends on:   0004 (the DTS base) and 0043 (it anchors after the last
              doorbell node, and shares 0043's UIO cmdline plumbing).
              Patch 0045 is what makes the write-combining property do
              anything; this patch is correct and inert without it.

Signed-off-by: Michael C. Ferguson <michael.christopher.ferguson@gmail.com>
---
 .../boot/dts/intel/socfpga/socfpga_cyclone5_de10nano.dts | 121 +++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 121 insertions(+)

--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/intel/socfpga/socfpga_cyclone5_de10nano.dts
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/intel/socfpga/socfpga_cyclone5_de10nano.dts
@@ -280,6 +280,127 @@
 		interrupt-parent = <&intc>;
 		interrupts = <0 55 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;	/* f2h_irq15 */
 	};
+
+	/*
+	 * Reg-bearing UIO nodes: named, bounded, descriptor-based mappings of
+	 * the two FPGA-side windows, so the HAL maps them through /dev/uioN
+	 * instead of through /dev/mem.  Memory only -- these carry no
+	 * interrupts, the doorbell nodes above carry no reg, and the two
+	 * families stay orthogonal on purpose.  uio_pdrv_genirq accepts an
+	 * interrupt-less node: platform_get_irq_optional() returns -ENXIO and
+	 * the device runs with irq = UIO_IRQ_NONE.
+	 *
+	 * The exported name is the ABI, and here it is stated explicitly
+	 * rather than derived: uio_pdrv_genirq uses "linux,uio-name" verbatim
+	 * for /sys/class/uio/uioN/name when the property is present, and falls
+	 * back to the fwnode name ("%pfwP", unit address included) only when
+	 * it is absent.  That is what lets these nodes carry a DT-correct unit
+	 * address -- wanted for any node with "reg", and warned about by dtc's
+	 * unit_address_vs_reg check (default-on in standalone dtc; in a kernel
+	 * build only under W=1) -- while userspace still matches the bare
+	 * string.  Userspace must never key on the uioN minor: adding any UIO
+	 * node shifts the numbering of every node after it.
+	 *
+	 * MAP_SHARED, ALWAYS.  UIO does not check the mapping flags -- there
+	 * is no VM_SHARED test anywhere in drivers/uio/uio.c -- so a
+	 * MAP_PRIVATE mmap of either node is accepted, silently COWs on the
+	 * first write, and diverts every subsequent store into anonymous RAM
+	 * the FPGA can never see.  Reads still return real memory and a
+	 * read-back self-test through the same mapping still passes, so the
+	 * corruption is invisible from the process that caused it.
+	 *
+	 * mister_lw_window -- the whole 2 MB lightweight HPS-to-FPGA bridge
+	 * window.  Core control registers and doorbell cause registers live
+	 * here.  This node must ALWAYS map strongly-ordered: uio_mmap_physical
+	 * applies pgprot_noncached unconditionally to UIO_MEM_PHYS, which is
+	 * exactly right here, not a limitation to work around.  The GPO strobe
+	 * handshake and the register-window fence bracketing depend on it.
+	 * NEVER add mister,map-writecombine to this node -- the reason the two
+	 * windows are separate nodes at all is that the property is
+	 * node-scoped, so this one is structurally incapable of inheriting it.
+	 * That rule is not only about ordering, and the ordering half is the
+	 * weaker one: write-combining is Normal memory, and ARMv7 permits
+	 * SPECULATIVE reads from Normal memory while forbidding them to
+	 * Strongly-ordered memory.  A WC mapping of a window where undecoded
+	 * reads hard-hang the board (H-1, below) could therefore hang it off a
+	 * mispredicted path, with the program never issuing a bad access at
+	 * all.  Mapping this window is safe only BECAUSE it is strongly
+	 * ordered.  Fencing correctly does not buy back the right to relax it.
+	 *
+	 * MAP-ONLY, NEVER PROBE (hardware finding H-1).  Mapping this window
+	 * is safe.  Touching an offset that no fabric slave decodes is not:
+	 * the interconnect waits forever for a response and the HPS hard-hangs
+	 * with no exception, no panic, no console output and no crash record
+	 * -- power-cycle-only recovery, confirmed three times on silicon,
+	 * including once with a serial console attached that logged nothing.
+	 * All three confirmed events were READS; no write to an undecoded
+	 * offset has been issued on hardware, but a write awaits a fabric
+	 * response through this mapping too, so presume it equally lethal --
+	 * the rule covers every access type, not just the measured one.
+	 * The menu core decodes nothing in this window at all.  A consumer may
+	 * therefore touch only offsets that a loaded core has declared, and
+	 * "probe the window to see what is there" is a destructive operation,
+	 * diagnostics included.
+	 *
+	 * mister_ddr_aperture -- the 512 MiB DDR3 aperture the FPGA shares
+	 * with the HPS through the f2sdram ports (0x20000000..0x3FFFFFFF).
+	 * The kernel does not own this memory and cannot: the boot command
+	 * line caps it at mem=511M, so every page here is outside the kernel
+	 * memory map (docs/boot-chain.md section 6.4).  That is also why
+	 * /dev/mem cannot help -- for !pfn_valid ranges ARM's
+	 * phys_mem_access_prot() returns pgprot_noncached whatever the flags,
+	 * O_SYNC included.
+	 *
+	 * That cap is a PRECONDITION of the write-combining map below, not
+	 * just an explanation.  memory@0 above still declares the full 1 GiB,
+	 * and mem=511M -- supplied by stock U-Boot, which this tree does not
+	 * own -- is the only thing holding these pages out of the kernel's
+	 * linear map.  Lose it and the WC mapping becomes a Normal
+	 * non-cacheable alias of pages the kernel also maps cacheable, which
+	 * ARMv7 leaves architecturally UNPREDICTABLE.  If that dependency ever
+	 * needs removing, do it with a /reserved-memory no-map node over this
+	 * range rather than by trusting the bootarg.
+	 *
+	 * "mister,map-writecombine" asks for a write-combining mapping of this
+	 * node's regions instead.  It is parsed only by patch 0045; on a
+	 * kernel without it the property is unknown and simply ignored, and
+	 * the node maps at today's strongly-ordered floor (~100 MB/s write,
+	 * measured).  The failure direction is deliberate: an old kernel with
+	 * a new DTB degrades to slow-and-correct, never to a CACHED mapping of
+	 * memory the FPGA writes without snooping the A9 caches -- that would
+	 * be silent corruption rather than lost throughput.
+	 *
+	 * Write-combining is a weaker ordering, so it moves work to the
+	 * consumer: a store into a WC region is not globally visible when the
+	 * instruction retires, and every publish-then-signal sequence must
+	 * fence before it rings a doorbell or raises a strobe.
+	 *
+	 * One map per node, and the whole aperture in it, because UIO selects
+	 * a map by page offset and always maps from the region base, so a
+	 * consumer reaching a high region maps a long prefix of address space
+	 * (VA only -- these are VM_PFNMAP, they cost no RAM and are not
+	 * page-locked by mlockall).  If that VA cost ever proves to matter,
+	 * split this node's reg into up to MAX_UIO_MAPS (5) entries at the hot
+	 * region bases; the property stays node-scoped either way.
+	 *
+	 * Both nodes sit at the root, beside the doorbell nodes, not under the
+	 * soc/bus@ff200000 node (label &fpga) -- that one is reserved for the
+	 * FPGA image's own DT overlay (see its comment), and nothing here
+	 * should depend on it.  The overlapping reg is not a conflict: neither
+	 * simple-bus nor uio_pdrv_genirq requests the region, they record it.
+	 */
+	mister_lw_window@ff200000 {
+		compatible = "generic-uio";
+		linux,uio-name = "mister_lw_window";
+		reg = <0xff200000 0x00200000>;
+	};
+
+	mister_ddr_aperture@20000000 {
+		compatible = "generic-uio";
+		linux,uio-name = "mister_ddr_aperture";
+		reg = <0x20000000 0x20000000>;
+		mister,map-writecombine;
+	};
 };
 
